 Yes (since \\(a=1\\))            | No                             |\n| \\((2,2)\\)       | No                             | No                             | Yes (since \\(a=b\\))            |\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) appears in at least one of the three rows, hence the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Determination of sunny lines  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=0),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=0),\\qquad x+y=0\\;( \\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\n- \\(\\ell_{1}: y=1\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (slope \\(0\\)); therefore it is **not sunny**.  \n- \\(\\ell_{2}: x=1\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical); therefore it is **not sunny**.  \n- \\(\\ell_{3}: y=x\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), and \\(-1\\); consequently \\(\\ell_{3}\\) is **sunny**.\n\nThus exactly one of the three lines is sunny, i.e. \\(k=1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited three distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) that\n\n1. cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le 4\\), and  \n2. contain precisely one sunny line.\n\nHence for \\(n=3\\) the integer \\(k=1\\) is attainable. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
