            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{A(1,1),\\;B(1,2),\\;C(1,3),\\;D(2,1),\\;E(2,2),\\;F(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the three lines.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\;\\neq\\infty,\\;\\neq-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Sunny pairs\n\nFor each unordered pair of points we compute the slope:\n\n| Pair | Slope |\n|------|-------|\n| \\(A\\!-\\!E\\) | \\(1\\) |\n| \\(B\\!-\\!F\\) | \\(-\\frac12\\) |\n| \\(C\\!-\\!D\\) | \\(-2\\) |\n| all other pairs | \\(0,\\;\\infty,\\) or \\(-1\\) |\n\nThus the only pairs that can lie on a sunny line are  \n\n\\[\nS_{1}=\\{A,E\\},\\qquad S_{2}=\\{B,F\\},\\qquad S_{3}=\\{C,D\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequences:\n\n* A sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(P\\).\n* If it contains two points, they must be one of the three sets \\(S_{1},S_{2},S_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Required distribution of points\n\nWe need three distinct lines covering all six points, with exactly two of them sunny.  \nThe third line must be non‑sunny, i.e. horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)), or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nWe consider all possible ways the six points can be allocated among the three lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1 Both sunny lines contain a pair\n\nChoose two disjoint sunny pairs from \\(\\{S_{1},S_{2},S_{3}\\}\\).  \nThe three possibilities are:\n\n1. \\(S_{1}\\) and \\(S_{2}\\) used → uncovered points \\(C,D\\).  \n   Slope of \\(CD\\) is \\(-2\\) (sunny).\n2. \\(S_{1}\\) and \\(S_{3}\\) used → uncovered points \\(B,F\\).  \n   Slope of \\(BF\\) is \\(-\\frac12\\) (sunny).\n3. \\(S_{2}\\) and \\(S_{3}\\) used → uncovered points \\(A,E\\).  \n   Slope of \\(AE\\) is \\(1\\) (sunny).\n\nIn each case the two remaining points lie on a sunny line, contradicting the requirement that the third line be non‑sunny.  \nHence this distribution is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2 One sunny line contains a pair, the other a single point\n\nAssume the sunny pair is \\(S_{1}=\\{A,E\\}\\).  \nThe other four points are \\(B,C,D,F\\).  \nOne of them will be placed on the second sunny line (as a single point); the remaining three must lie on a non‑sunny line.\n\nAll triples among \\(\\{B,C,D,F\\}\\) are examined:\n\n* \\(\\{B,C,D\\}\\): two points share \\(x=1\\) but the third does not, so not collinear.\n* \\(\\{B,C,F\\}\\): two share \\(x=1\\) but the third does not.\n* \\(\\{B,D,F\\}\\): slopes \\(BD=-1\\), \\(BF=-\\frac12\\), \\(DF=0\\); not equal.\n* \\(\\{C,D,F\\}\\): slopes \\(CD=-2\\), \\(CF=-1\\), \\(DF=0\\); not equal.\n\nThus no three of the remaining points are collinear with slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nThe same verification for the other two possible sunny pairs \\(S_{2}\\) and \\(S_{3}\\) yields identical conclusions.  \nTherefore this case cannot be realized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3 Both sunny lines contain a single point\n\nThen the non‑sunny line must contain the other four points.  \nThe longest horizontal line through points of \\(P\\) is \\(y=1\\), containing \\(A,D,F\\) (three points).  \nThe longest vertical line is \\(x=1\\), containing \\(A,B,C\\) (three points).  \nLines of slope \\(-1\\) are of the form \\(x+y=c\\); among them the most populated is \\(x+y=4\\), containing \\(C,E,F\\) (three points).  \nHence no horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\) line passes through four points of \\(P\\).  \nThus this distribution is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nAll possible allocations of the six required points among two sunny lines and one non‑sunny line lead to contradictions. Consequently there is **no** set of three distinct lines covering the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le4\\) that contains exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
