            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe are asked for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) **distinct** lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny*, i.e. their slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  \nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  No configuration can contain a sunny line\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) satisfying the covering condition contains at least one sunny line.\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the *column*\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |C_a|=n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for each \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) define the *row*\n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |R_b|=n+1-b .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nIf a column \\(C_a\\) is **not** covered by a vertical line, then every point of \\(C_a\\) must be covered by a distinct sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA line that is not vertical meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.  \nAmong the unsunny lines, the only ones that are not vertical are horizontal lines (slope \\(0\\)).  \nSince a sunny line is defined to have slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), a sunny line cannot be horizontal.  \nConsequently, if \\(C_a\\) lacks a vertical covering line, each of its \\(|C_a|\\) points needs a different sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a row \\(R_b\\) is **not** covered by a horizontal line, then every point of \\(R_b\\) must be covered by a distinct sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  The argument is symmetric to Lemma 1, using the fact that a non‑horizontal line meets the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point, and that the only unsunny lines that are not horizontal are vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nNow consider the column \\(C_1\\). Its size is \\(|C_1|=n\\), the largest among all columns.  \n\n*Case 1.*  The line \\(x=1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThen \\(C_1\\) is already covered, and no sunny line is forced by Lemma 1.\n\n*Case 2.*  The line \\(x=1\\) is **not** in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nBy Lemma 1, each of the \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) must be covered by a different sunny line.  \nHence at least \\(n\\) sunny lines are required.  \nSince the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\), this forces **all** the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) to be sunny.\n\nBut a sunny line is not vertical; therefore it can intersect the column \\(x=1\\) in at most one point.  \nWith all \\(n\\) lines sunny, at most \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) could be covered, and each line would cover **exactly one** point of \\(C_1\\).  \nConsequently the remaining points of \\(C_1\\) would be uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nThus Case 2 is impossible, and we must be in Case 1: the line \\(x=1\\) is present in the family.\n\nApply the same reasoning to the column \\(x=2\\).  \nIf the line \\(x=2\\) were missing, Lemma 1 would again demand at least \\(|C_2|=n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nTogether with the already present vertical line \\(x=1\\) we would have at least \\(1+(n-1)=n\\) lines, all of which would have to be sunny except possibly \\(x=1\\).  \nAgain a sunny line cannot be vertical, so the column \\(x=2\\) would contain uncovered points – contradiction.  \n\nHence the line \\(x=2\\) must also belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nRepeating this argument for \\(x=3,\\dots ,x=n\\) shows that **every** vertical line \\(x=a\\) \\((1\\le a\\le n)\\) must be among the \\(n\\) chosen lines.  \nTherefore the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).\n\nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. they are unsunny.  \nConsequently the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration is **zero**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited a construction with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines, and we have proved that any configuration satisfying the covering condition must contain no sunny line at all.  \n\nHence, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the only possible value of }k\\text{ is }0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task 