ide length \\(k\\); explicitly  \n\n   \\[\n   R_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |R_{n,k}| = N_n-\\sum_{b=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-b)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 3 – pairing the points.*  \n   List the points of \\(R_{n,k}\\) by increasing \\(a\\):\n\n   \\[\n   (1,n-k+1),\\;(1,n-k+2),\\dots ,(1,n),\\;\n   (2,n-k+1),\\dots ,(2,n-1),\\;\\dots ,\\;(k, n-k+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Pair them as follows (the construction works for every \\(k\\ge1\\)):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: (1,n-k+1)\\;\\text{with}\\;(2,n-k+2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: (1,n-k+2)\\;\\text{with}\\;(3,n-k+3),\\\\\n   &\\ \\vdots\\\\\n   L_{k-1}&: (1,n-1)\\;\\text{with}\\;(k,n),\\\\\n   L_k &: (1,n)\\;\\text{alone (if \\(k\\) is odd)}\\;\\text{or paired with}\\;(k,n-k+1)\\;\\text{(if \\(k\\) is even)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Each pair consists of two distinct points with different \\(x\\)‑ and \\(y\\)‑coordinates, and the slope of the line through a pair is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore every line \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   When \\(k\\) is odd the last line \\(L_k\\) contains a single point; we simply let it be the line through that point with any sunny direction (for instance slope \\(2\\)). Thus we obtain exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each meeting \\(R_{n,k}\\) in at least one point and never meeting any of the horizontal lines (2).\n\n   *Step 4 – verification.*  \n   - The \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (2) cover all points with \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \n   - The \\(k\\) sunny lines (5) cover every point of \\(R_{n,k}\\).  \n   - All \\(n\\) lines are distinct.  \n   - Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration satisfying the two requirements exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) (Lemma 1 + inequality (1)) with the explicit construction for all smaller \\(k\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \nN_n=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\) on each horizontal line \\(y=c\\), on each vertical line \\(x=c\\) and on each anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) met a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) in two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) it would be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, contradicting the definition of sunny. The same argument works for vertical lines and for the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); a second intersection with a line of the family \\(x+y=s\\) would make \\(\\ell\\) parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). ∎\n\nConsequences of Lemma 1:\n\n* a sunny line meets at most \\(n\\) different rows, at most \\(n\\) different columns and at most \\(n\\) different anti‑diagonals;\n* therefore it contains at most  \n\n\\[\nm_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  \nIndeed, the rows of \\(T_n\\) have lengths \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,1\\).  \nBecause a sunny line can meet each row at most once, the largest possible number of points it can contain is obtained by taking the longest rows, i.e. the first \\(m_n\\) rows, giving exactly \\(m_n\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nUsing the bound (6) for each of them we obtain\n\n\\[\nn\\cdot m_n\\ge N_n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, say \\(n=2t\\), then \\(m_n=t\\) and the left–hand side of (7) equals \\(n\\cdot t=n^2/2\\), whereas  \n\n\\[\nN_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=\\frac{2t(2t+1)}2=t(2t+1)=\\frac{n^2}{2}+ \\frac{n}{2}> \\frac{n^2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus (7) fails for even \\(n\\).\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, write \\(n=2t+1\\). Then \\(m_n=t+1\\) and (7) becomes\n\n\\[\n(2t+1)(t+1)\\ge\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2,\n\\]\n\ni.e. equality. Equality would require **every** sunny line to contain exactly \\(t+1\\) points, and those points would have to lie in the first \\(t+1\\) rows. Consequently each of the \\(t+1\\) rows would be intersected by **all** \\(n\\) lines, which is impossible because a line can meet a given row at most once (Lemma 1). Hence (7) cannot hold for odd \\(n\\) either.\n\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n#### 3.1 Horizontal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\,}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct, non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n#### 3.2 The uncovered triangle  \n\nThe points that are still uncovered form the set  \n\n\\[\nR_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a\\) for the horizontal coordinate, one checks that  \n\n\\[\nR_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(R_{n,k}\\) is a right‑angled integer triangle of side length \\(k\\); a direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|R_{n,k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.3 Sunny lines covering \\(R_{n,k}\\)\n\nWe now produce \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(R_{n,k}\\).\n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i :\\text{ the line through }(1,n-k+i)\\text{ and }(i+1,n-k+i+1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe two points have different \\(x\\)‑ and \\(y\\)‑coordinates, and their slope equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-k+i+1)-(n-k+i)}{(i+1)-1}= \\frac{1}{i},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, the remaining points of \\(R_{n,k}\\) are exactly the \\(k/2\\) pairs  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(1,n\\bigr)\\text{ with }\\bigl(k,n-k+1\\bigr),\\;\n\\bigl(2,n-1\\bigr)\\text{ with }\\bigl(k-1,n-k+2\\bigr),\\dots\n\\]\n\nand we define for \\(i=k/2\\)\n\n\\[\nL_{k}: \\text{ the line through }(i,n+1-i)\\text{ and }(k+1-i,n+1-(k+1-i)).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAgain the slope is \\(-\\dfrac{i}{k+1-i}\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\), so \\(L_k\\) is sunny.\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd, after the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) there remains a single point,\n\\((1,n)\\). Choose any sunny direction, e.g. slope \\(2\\), and let  \n\n\\[\nL_k :\\; y- n = 2\\,(x-1).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus we have produced exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each meeting \\(R_{n,k}\\) in at least one point and never intersecting any of the horizontal lines (8).\n\n#### 3.4 Verification\n\n* Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to a horizontal line (8) or to the triangle \\(R_{n,k}\\); the latter are covered by the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \n* The \\(n\\) lines are pairwise distinct (the horizontals have different slopes from the sunny lines, and the sunny lines have distinct slopes because the slopes in (11) are \\(\\frac1i\\) for different \\(i\\)).  \n* Exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, namely the \\(L_i\\).\n\nHence a configuration satisfying the two requirements exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) (Section 2) with the construction for all smaller \\(k\\) (Section 3) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-1.}\n\\]"
