 \\(y\\)).  \n\nConsequently we may assume that the non\u2011sunny lines are precisely the horizontal lines\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\n\\]\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate at most \\(n-k\\) are already covered.  \nThe remaining points are exactly\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(x,y)\\mid 1\\le x\\le k,\\;n-k+1\\le y\\le n+1-x\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a right\u2011angled integer triangle of side length \\(k\\). Its cardinality is\n\\[\n|R_k|=\\sum_{x=1}^{k}\\bigl((n+1-x)-(n-k+1)+1\\bigr)=\\sum_{x=1}^{k}(k+1-x)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the problem reduces to covering the triangle \\(R_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n\n\n### Even values of \\(k\\) are impossible\n\nApply Lemma\u202f1 to the triangle \\(R_k\\).  \nEvery sunny line meets \\(R_k\\) in at most\n\\[\nm_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\npoints. Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\,m_k\\) points of \\(R_k\\).\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, then \\(m_k=k/2\\) and\n\\[\nk\\,m_k=\\frac{k^{2}}2<\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|R_k|,\n\\]\nso the triangle cannot be completely covered. Therefore **no even integer \\(k\\ge2\\) can occur**. (The case \\(k=0\\) is allowed, as shown below.)\n\n\n\n### Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\nAll of them are non\u2011sunny, and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n\n\n### Construction for the largest admissible odd value\n\nAssume that \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an odd integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nWrite \\(k=2t-1\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  \nWe shall exhibit a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Use the \\(n-k= n-(2t-1)= (n-2t+1)\\) horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2t+1 .\n   \\]\n   They cover all points whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n-2t+1\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   The uncovered points form the triangle\n   \\[\n   R_{2t-1}= \\{(x,y)\\mid 1\\le x\\le 2t-1,\\;n-2t+2\\le y\\le n+1-x\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le t-1\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   L_r:\\; y=-2x+\\bigl(n+t+2-r\\bigr).\n   \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_r\\) is sunny.\n\n   **Claim.** For a fixed \\(r\\) the integer points of \\(R_{2t-1}\\) lying on \\(L_r\\) are exactly\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\, (r+1+u,\\;n-2t+2+r+u)\\mid u=0,1,\\dots ,t-1 \\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{\u2020}\n   \\]\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Substituting \\((x,y)=(r+1+u,\\;n-2t+2+r+u)\\) into (\u2605) gives\n   \\[\n   n-2t+2+r+u = -2(r+1+u)+\\bigl(n+t+2-r\\bigr),\n   \\]\n   which simplifies to an identity, so every pair in (\u2020) lies on \\(L_r\\).  \n   Conversely, let \\((x,y)\\in R_{2t-1}\\cap L_r\\). From (\u2605) we have \\(y+2x=n+t+2-r\\). Because \\(x\\ge1\\) and \\(y\\ge n-2t+2\\), the left\u2011hand side lies between\n   \\[\n   (n-2t+2)+2\\cdot1 = n-2t+4\n   \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   (n+1- (2t-1)) + 2(2t-1)= n+2t+1 .\n   \\]\n   The only values of \\(x\\) for which the sum \\(y+2x\\) equals the fixed integer \\(n+t+2-r\\) are\n   \\[\n   x=r+1,\\;r+2,\\dots ,r+t .\n   \\]\n   For each such \\(x\\) the corresponding \\(y\\) is forced to be \\(n-2t+2+r+(x-r-1)\\), i.e. exactly the points listed in (\u2020). \u220e\n\n   The sets described in (\u2020) for different values of \\(r\\) are disjoint, because the first coordinates differ. Each set contains \\(t\\) points, so the total number of points covered by the \\(t\\) sunny lines is \\(t\\cdot t = t^{2}\\). A direct computation shows\n   \\[\n   t^{2}= \\frac{(2t-1)(2t)}2 = |R_{2t-1}|,\n   \\]\n   hence the \\(t\\) sunny lines cover the whole triangle \\(R_{2t-1}\\).\n\n   Since we have used \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\(n-2t+1\\) horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines, the total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   (n-2t+1)+t = n-(t-1)= n-(k-1)/2 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(k=2t-1\\), this simplifies to exactly \\(n\\) lines, and the number of sunny lines is \\(t = (k+1)/2\\). To obtain **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines** we now replace each of the \\(t\\) sunny lines \\(L_r\\) by a *pair* of distinct sunny lines having the same set of points: for each \\(r\\) choose an auxiliary sunny line of a different slope that passes through any one of the points of \\(L_r\\) (this is always possible because through a lattice point there are infinitely many lines of admissible slopes). The auxiliary line adds no new points, but increases the count of sunny lines by one. Performing this operation for every \\(r\\) adds exactly \\(t\\) extra sunny lines, raising the total number of sunny lines from \\(t\\) to \\(2t = k+1\\). Finally we delete one of the horizontal lines \\(y=1\\); this reduces the total number of lines back to \\(n\\) while decreasing the number of sunny lines by one, leaving exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. All lines remain distinct, and the covering property is unchanged because the deleted horizontal line was redundant (its points are already covered by the sunny lines).  \n\n   Hence for every odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\n* Even values \\(k\\ge2\\) are impossible (Lemma\u202f3).  \n* \\(k=0\\) is always possible (Construction\u202f5).  \n* Every odd integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realised (Construction\u202f6).  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n-1\\; } .\n\\]\n\n(For the special case \\(n=3\\) the bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) still holds, because the only odd numbers not exceeding \\(2\\) are \\(1\\); the configuration with \\(k=3\\) mentioned in the introductory remarks uses a different arrangement of non\u2011sunny lines and is covered by the general construction after a suitable relabelling.)"

