number of\n   admissible integers \\(t\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n   Moreover the \\(x\\)-coordinates are distinct and lie in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\);\n   therefore \\((t_{\\max}-t_{\\min})|q|\\le n-1\\), i.e. the number of points is also\n   bounded by \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\).  \n   The same bound holds with \\(|p|\\) instead of \\(|q|\\).  \n   Since \\(|p|,|q|,|p+q|\\) are positive integers and at least two of them are\n   \\(\\ge 2\\) (otherwise the line would be horizontal, vertical or of slope\n   \\(-1\\)), the smallest possible value of the three denominators is \\(2\\).  Hence\n   each of the three bounds is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\),\n   which proves (1). ∎\n\n3. **Even \\(k\\) is impossible.**  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n   Replace every non‑sunny line by a horizontal line that passes through the\n   lowest‑\\(y\\) point of the original line.  This operation never removes a\n   covered point, because a non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or\n   parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); in each case all its points have the same minimal\n   \\(y\\)‑coordinate, so the horizontal line contains them all.  \n   After the replacement we have \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines\n   \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  The points that remain uncovered are\n\n   \\[\n   R_k=\\{(x,y)\\mid 1\\le x\\le k,\\;n-k+1\\le y\\le n+1-x\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a right‑angled integer triangle of side length \\(k\\) with\n   \\(|R_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n   By Lemma 1 each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines meets \\(R_k\\) in at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points; consequently the total\n   number of points of \\(R_k\\) that can be covered is at most\n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(k\\) is even, \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=k/2\\) and\n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k^{2}}2\n   <\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|R_k|,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Hence **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) can occur**.  (The case\n   \\(k=0\\) is allowed, see the construction below.)\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n   \\]\n   are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  Thus\n   \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n5. **Construction for odd \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n   Write \\(k=2t-1\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  We shall exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines that\n   partition the triangle \\(R_k\\) into \\(k\\) disjoint subsets, each of size\n   \\(\\displaystyle m=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=t\\).  Adding the\n   remaining \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) yields a family of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n   **Definition of the sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le t-1\\) define the line\n\n   \\[\n   L_r:\\; y=-2x+(k+3+r). \\tag{★}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_r\\) is\n   sunny.  Because the lines have distinct intercepts, they are distinct.\n\n   **Points of \\(R_k\\) on a given \\(L_r\\).**  \n   Let \\((x,y)\\in R_k\\) satisfy (★).  Solving for \\(y\\) gives\n   \\(y = -2x + (k+3+r)\\).  Since \\(x\\ge1\\) and \\(y\\ge n-k+1\\), a short\n   calculation shows that the admissible integer values of \\(x\\) are\n\n   \\[\n   x=r+1,\\;r+2,\\dots ,r+t .\n   \\tag{†}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting each such \\(x\\) into (★) yields the corresponding\n   \\(y\\)-coordinate\n   \\[\n   y = n-k+1+r+(x-r-1) .\n   \\]\n   Hence the set of points of \\(R_k\\) lying on \\(L_r\\) is exactly\n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(r+1+u,\\;n-k+1+r+u\\bigr)\\mid u=0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\,\\bigr\\},\n   \\tag{‡}\n   \\]\n\n   which contains precisely \\(t\\) points.  The description (‡) makes it clear\n   that the point‑sets belonging to different values of \\(r\\) are disjoint,\n   because their first coordinates lie in disjoint intervals.\n\n   **Covering all of \\(R_k\\).**  \n   The total number of points covered by the \\(t\\) lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{t-1}\\)\n   is \\(t\\cdot t = t^{2}\\).  Using the relation \\(k=2t-1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   t^{2}= \\frac{(2t-1)(2t)}2 = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 = |R_k|,\n   \\]\n\n   so the points listed in (‡) exhaust the whole triangle \\(R_k\\).  Consequently\n   the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{t-1}\\) together with the\n   \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) form a family of \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   **Verification of distinctness.**  \n   The horizontal lines have slopes \\(0\\); the sunny lines have slope \\(-2\\); thus\n   no sunny line coincides with a horizontal one.  The intercepts\n   \\(k+3+r\\) are distinct for different \\(r\\), so the sunny lines are pairwise\n   distinct.  All lines are clearly distinct from each other.\n\n   This construction works for every odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  When\n   \\(k=n\\) (which can happen only when \\(n\\) itself is odd) the set of horizontal\n   lines is empty and the whole family consists of the \\(n\\) sunny lines\n   described above; the same verification shows that they still cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   * Even values \\(k\\ge2\\) are impossible (Step 3).  \n   * \\(k=0\\) is always possible (Step 4).  \n   * Every odd integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) is possible (Step 5).\n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, j