"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (Maximum points of a sunny line in the \u201cupper\u2011right\u2019\u2019 triangle).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   R_k=\\bigl\\{(x,y)\\mid 1\\le x\\le k,\\;n-k+1\\le y\\le n+1-x\\bigr\\},\n   \\qquad |R_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For any sunny line \\(\\ell\\) one has  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap R_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) in reduced rational form \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Q\\), \\(b\\in\\mathbb Q\\).  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) contains integer points, \\(m\\) is rational; write \\(m=p/q\\) with\n   coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \n   The \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 condition means \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).\n\n   An integer point \\((x,y)\\) on \\(\\ell\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   y= \\frac{p}{q}x+b\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad qy=px+qb .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(x\\equiv x_0\\pmod q\\) for a fixed residue \\(x_0\\); consequently among the\n   integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) there are at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil k/q\\bigr\\rceil\\) values of\n   \\(x\\) that can belong to \\(\\ell\\).\n\n   *Case \\(q\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Then \\(\\bigl\\lceil k/q\\bigr\\rceil\\le\\bigl\\lceil k/2\\bigr\\rceil\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), and (1) holds.\n\n   *Case \\(q=1\\) (integral slope).*  \n   Here \\(m=p\\) with \\(p\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   For a point of \\(R_k\\) we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k+1\\le px+b\\le n+1-x . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (2) for \\(x\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n-k+1-b}{p}\\le x\\le\\frac{n+1-b}{p+1}\\qquad(p>0),\n   \\]\n   or the analogous inequalities when \\(p\\le -2\\).  \n   In either sub\u2011case the length of the admissible integer interval for \\(x\\) is at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Hence (1) also holds when \\(q=1\\).\n\n   Thus every sunny line meets \\(R_k\\) in at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n3. **Necessary condition for \\(k\\).**  \n   Start with any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\)\n   and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Replace each non\u2011sunny line by a horizontal line that passes through the\n   *lowest* point of the original line.  \n   Because a non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or parallel to\n   \\(x+y=0\\), the lowest point has the smallest \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate among the points\n   of that line; the horizontal line through it contains **all** points of the\n   original line (the other two families are monotone in the \\(y\\)\u2011direction).  \n   Hence the replacement does not destroy any covered point.  \n   After the replacement we have\n\n   * \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\);\n   * the same \\(k\\) sunny lines as before.\n\n   The points not lying on any of the horizontal lines are exactly the set \\(R_k\\)\n   defined above; \\(|R_k|=k(k+1)/2\\).\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(R_k\\).  Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2=|R_k|\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2011hand side of (3) equals \\(\\dfrac{k^2}{2}\\) when \\(k\\) is even and\n   \\(\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\) when \\(k\\) is odd.  Consequently (3) can hold only if\n   \\(k\\) is odd or \\(k=0\\).  Hence\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd}\\; } . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sufficiency for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be odd, write \\(k=2t-1\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  \n   Consider the following family of \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n   * Horizontal lines  \n     \\[\n     H_i:\\; y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n     \\]\n\n   * Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)  \n     \\[\n     S_j:\\; y = x + (n-k+j-1)\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   The \\(k\\) lines in (5) are sunny because their slope is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\),\n   and they are pairwise distinct (different intercepts).\n\n   **Coverage of the lower part.**  \n   The horizontal lines \\(H_i\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(y\\le n-k\\).\n\n   **Coverage of the upper\u2011right triangle \\(R_k\\).**  \n   Take a point \\((x,y)\\in R_k\\).  By definition \\(1\\le x\\le k\\) and\n   \\(n-k+1\\le y\\le n+1-x\\).  Set \\(d=y-x\\).  From the inequalities we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\le d\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(d=n-k+j-1\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), and the point satisfies\n   \\(y = x + (n-k+j-1)\\), i.e. it lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(R_k\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(S_j\\).\n   Consequently the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered by the \\(n\\) lines described\n   above, and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   The construction works for every odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n5. **The case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are all non\u2011sunny and clearly\n   cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nCombining the necessary condition (4) with the explicit constructions for\n\\(k=0\\) and for every odd \\(k\\le n\\) yields the complete description of the\nadmissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]"
