            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reducing the problem to a triangle\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nChoose the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\) lies on\none of the lines (1).  \nThe points that are **not** covered by (1) are exactly\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nobtained by the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\).  \nThus the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  For a given \\(k\\) find \\(k\\) sunny lines that together\n> contain all points of the triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\nIf we succeed for a certain \\(k\\), adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) gives a\nsolution of the original problem with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Covering the triangle \\(T_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0,1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take no sunny line; the vertical lines (1) already cover all of\n  \\(S_n\\).\n* \\(k=1\\): keep the vertical lines (1) and add any sunny line, for instance\n  \\(y=2x+1\\); it meets at least one point of \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nDefine, for each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,k+2-i).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth points satisfy (2) because \\(i+1\\le k+1\\) and\n\\(1+(k+2-i)=k+3-i\\le k+1\\) holds exactly when \\(i\\ge2\\); the point\n\\(Q_1=(1,k+1)\\) also belongs to \\(T_k\\).  \nLet \\(L_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).\n\n*Slope.*  From (3)\n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{k+1-i}{1-i}= -\\,\\frac{k+1-i}{i-1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(i\\)\n\n* \\(m_i\\neq\\infty\\) because \\(i\\neq1\\);\n* \\(m_i\\neq0\\) because \\(k+1-i\\neq0\\) (otherwise \\(i=k+1>k\\));\n* \\(m_i=-1\\) would give \\(\\frac{k+1-i}{i-1}=1\\), i.e. \\(k=2\\) and \\(i=2\\).\n  The case \\(k=2\\) is handled separately (see § 2.3).\n\nHence for all \\(k\\ge3\\) each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe lines are distinct because the unordered pairs \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) are distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le b\\)*, then \\(b\\ge\\lceil\\frac{k+2}{2}\\rceil\\) and\n\\(a=k+2-b\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)=Q_b\\) and the point lies on \\(L_b\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge b\\)*, then \\(a\\ge\\lceil\\frac{k+2}{2}\\rceil\\) and\n\\(b=k+2-a\\).  Hence \\((a,b)=P_a\\) and the point lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\nThus every lattice point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n\\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\).\n\n#### 2.3  The special triangle \\(T_2\\)\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nTwo sunny lines covering it are\n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1\\quad\\text{through }(1,1),\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=3x-2\\quad\\text{through }(2,1).\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); together they contain the\nthree points of \\(T_2\\).\n\nConsequently, for **every** \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we have produced\n\\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).  Adding the vertical lines (1) yields a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the original\nstatement and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny members.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nsunny lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Hypotenuse points.*  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\;1\\le a,b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points.  Any two points of \\(H\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore\na sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(H\\).  Because we have only\n\\(n\\) lines, each line must contain exactly one point of \\(H\\).\n\n*Left‑side points.*  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nalso has \\(n\\) points.  Two distinct points of \\(V\\) lie on a vertical line,\nwhich is non‑sunny; consequently a sunny line can contain at most one point of\n\\(V\\).  By the pigeon‑hole principle at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines\ncontains **no** point of \\(V\\).\n\nConsider such a line \\(\\ell\\).  It contains a unique point \\((a,n+1-a)\\in H\\)\nwith \\(a\\neq1\\).  If \\(\\ell\\) contained another admissible point\n\\((x,y)\\in S_n\\), then the slope of the segment joining \\((a,n+1-a)\\) and\n\\((x,y)\\) would be either \\(0\\) (if \\(y=n+1-a\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (if \\(x=a\\)) or\n\\(-1\\) (if \\(x+y=n+1\\)), all of which are forbidden for a sunny line.\nHence \\(\\ell\\) contains **exactly one** point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nAll the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines contain one point of \\(H\\) and at most one\npoint of \\(V\\); therefore each of them covers at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n\\) sunny\nlines is at most\n\n\\[\n1\\;+\\;(n-1)\\cdot 2 \\;=\\;2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (the case \\(n=2\\) is\nexcluded by the hypothesis \\(n\\ge3\\)).  This contradiction shows that a\nconfiguration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThe constructions of Sections 2.1–2.3 give explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines attaining each such \\(k\\); the impossibility for \\(k=n\\) follows from\nthe counting argument of Section 3.  This completes the proof.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
