"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nand, in addition, the value \\(k=n\\) is possible **only when \\(n=3\\)**.  \nThus the full answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{n\\}\\ \\text{if }n=3\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Reduction to a right\u2011angled lattice triangle.**  \n   Choose \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   They cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \n   The uncovered points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   a right\u2011angled lattice triangle of side\u2011length \\(k\\).  \n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to: *for a given \\(k\\) find \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines that contain every lattice point of \\(T_k\\)*.\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Case \\(k=0\\).*  No sunny line is needed; the \\(n\\) vertical lines already\n   cover all required points.  \n\n   *Case \\(k\\ge 1\\).*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,k+2-i)\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (both belong to \\(T_k\\)).  \n   Let \\(L_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  \n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{k+1-i}{1-i}= -\\frac{k+1-i}{\\,i-1\\,}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For every \\(i\\) we have  \n\n   * \\(m_i\\neq\\infty\\) because \\(i\\neq1\\);  \n   * \\(m_i\\neq0\\) because \\(k+1-i\\neq0\\) (otherwise \\(i=k+1>k\\));  \n   * \\(m_i\\neq-1\\) except when \\((k,i)=(2,2)\\).  \n\n   The exceptional case \\(k=2\\) is handled separately (see \u00a7\u202f2.3).  \n   Consequently, for all \\(k\\ge 3\\) each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are\n   distinct because the unordered pairs \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  \n   If \\(a\\le b\\) then \\(b=k+2-a\\) and \\((a,b)=Q_b\\); hence the point lies on\n   \\(L_b\\).  \n   If \\(a\\ge b\\) then \\(a=k+2-b\\) and \\((a,b)=P_a\\); hence the point lies on\n   \\(L_a\\).  \n   Thus every lattice point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n   \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\).\n\n   *Special case \\(k=2\\).*  \n   \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  Two sunny lines covering it are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1\\quad\\text{through }(1,1),\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;y=3x-2\\quad\\text{through }(2,1),\n   \\]\n\n   both of which have slopes \\(2\\) and \\(3\\), respectively.  \n\n   Adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Hence every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **The case \\(k=n\\).**  \n   We must decide for which \\(n\\) it is possible to cover all points with\n   *only* sunny lines.\n\n   *Existence for \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell_1:\\;y=x      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n   &\\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n   &\\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5   &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and together\n   contain all ten points with \\(a+b\\le4\\).  Hence \\(k=n=3\\) is possible.\n\n   *Impossibility for \\(n\\ge4\\).*  \n   Assume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n\n   - The **hypotenuse**  \n     \\[\n     H=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n     consists of \\(n\\) points.  Two distinct points of \\(H\\) lie on a line of\n     slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of\n     \\(H\\).  Consequently each of the \\(n\\) lines contains exactly one point of\n     \\(H\\).\n\n   - The **left side**  \n     \\[\n     L=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n     also has \\(n\\) points.  A sunny line cannot be vertical, so it can meet\n     \\(L\\) in at most one point.  Because we have only \\(n\\) lines, each line\n     must contain exactly one point of \\(L\\) as well; otherwise some line would\n     contain none and the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines would have to cover at least\n     \\(n\\) points of \\(L\\), impossible.\n\n   Hence we may label the lines \\(\\ell_i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) so that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i\\text{ contains }H_i=(i,n+1-i)\\text{ and }L_i=(1,b_i)\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   with the \\(b_i\\)\u2019s a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n   For \\(i\\ge 2\\) the line determined by \\(H_i\\) and \\(L_i\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{b_i-(n+1-i)}{1-i}\n        =\\frac{b_i+i-n-1}{1-i}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the line is sunny we must have \\(m_i\\neq-1\\).  The equality\n   \\(m_i=-1\\) is equivalent to \\(b_i=n+2-i\\).  Thus for every \\(i\\ge2\\) we must\n   have  \n\n   \\[\n   b_i\\neq n+2-i .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The numbers \\(n+2-i\\;(2\\le i\\le n)\\) form the set\n   \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Condition (8) therefore forces the permutation\n   \\((b_2,\\dots ,b_n)\\) to avoid the identity map on \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); i.e.\n   it is a derangement of that set.\n\n   For \\(i=1\\) the line \\(\\ell_1\\) cannot contain the point \\((1,n)\\) of \\(H\\)\n   together with any point of \\(L\\) (that would be vertical).  Hence \\(\\ell_1\\)\n   must contain a point of the **bottom side**  \n\n   \\[\n   B=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(\\ell_1\\) contain \\((a_1,1)\\) with \\(a_1\\neq1\\).  Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_1=\\frac{1-n}{a_1-1}= -\\frac{n-1}{a_1-1},\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   which is never \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\) because \\(a_1\\ge2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\); it is also\n   finite, so \\(\\ell_1\\) is indeed sunny.\n\n   Now consider the point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(L\\).  It must lie on some line\n   \\(\\ell_j\\) with \\(j\\ge2\\).  Since \\(\\ell_j\\) already contains the two points\n   \\(H_j\\) and \\(L_j\\), the three points \\((1,1),H_j,L_j\\) would have to be\n   collinear.  Using (7) the condition that \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_j\\) is\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1-(n+1-j)}{1-j}=m_j\n   \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   \\frac{j-n}{1-j}= \\frac{b_j+j-n-1}{1-j},\n   \\]\n\n   which simplifies to \\(b_j=1\\).  Thus the only line among\n   \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) that can contain \\((1,1)\\) is the one with\n   \\(b_j=1\\).\n\n   But the numbers \\(b_2,\\dots ,b_n\\) form a permutation of\n   \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{b_1\\}\\).  Since \\(b_1\\neq1\\) (the point\n   \\((1,1)\\) is not on \\(\\ell_1\\)), the value \\(1\\) must appear among\n   \\(b_2,\\dots ,b_n\\).  Consequently there is a unique index \\(j\\ge2\\) with\n   \\(b_j=1\\), and \\(\\ell_j\\) is forced to pass through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n   However, for this index \\(j\\) we have \\(b_j=1\\neq n+2-j\\) (because \\(n\\ge4\\)),\n   so (8) is satisfied.  The three points \\((1,1),H_j,L_j\\) are collinear,\n   which forces the slope \\(m_j\\) to be\n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{1-(n+1-j)}{1-j}= -\\frac{n-j}{\\,j-1\\,}.\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing (11) with (7) (where \\(b_j=1\\)) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{n-j}{j-1}= \\frac{1+j-n-1}{1-j}= -\\frac{n-j}{j-1},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the two expressions coincide, so no contradiction arises here.\n   The contradiction comes from counting the points of the bottom side\n   \\(B\\).\n\n   The bottom side contains \\(n\\) points.  The line \\(\\ell_1\\) already covers\n   exactly one of them, namely \\((a_1,1)\\) with \\(a_1\\ge2\\).  Each of the\n   remaining \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) can meet \\(B\\) in at most\n   one point (otherwise the line would be horizontal).  Hence at most\n   \\(1+(n-1)=n\\) points of \\(B\\) can be covered.  But the point \\((1,1)\\) is\n   already accounted for, so the total number of points of \\(B\\) covered is at\n   most \\(n-1\\).  Since \\(|B|=n\\), at least one point of the bottom side would\n   remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\n   Therefore a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist when\n   \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n4. **Collecting the results.**  \n   * For every \\(k\\le n-1\\) the construction of \u00a7\u202f2 yields a family of \\(n\\)\n     distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members.  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the explicit family of three sunny lines given in \u00a7\u202f3 shows\n     that \\(k=n\\) is also attainable.  \n   * For all \\(n\\ge4\\) the impossibility proof of \u00a7\u202f3 shows that \\(k=n\\) is\n     impossible.\n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{n\\}\\text{ if }n=3\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a triangle\n\nFix a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\).  \nChoose the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\) lies on\none of the lines (1).  The points that are not covered are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}, \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhich is a right\u2011angled lattice triangle of side\u2011length \\(k\\).  Consequently,\nthe original problem is equivalent to the following.\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) find \\(k\\) sunny lines that\n> together contain every lattice point of the triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\nIf such a family exists, adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) gives a family\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  Conversely, any solution of the original problem yields a\nsolution of \\(*\\) by deleting all non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for every \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\n#### 2.1  The cases \\(k=0,1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): no sunny line is needed; the vertical lines (1) already cover\n  \\(S_n\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep the vertical lines (1) and add any sunny line, for instance\n  \\(y=2x+1\\); it meets at least one point of \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,k+2-i). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth points satisfy (2) because \\(i+1\\le k+1\\) and\n\\(1+(k+2-i)=k+3-i\\le k+1\\) for \\(i\\ge2\\); for \\(i=1\\) we have\n\\(Q_1=(1,k+1)\\in T_k\\) as well.  Let \\(L_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and\n\\(Q_i\\).\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{k+1-i}{1-i}= -\\frac{k+1-i}{\\,i-1\\,}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(k\\ge3\\).*  \nFor every \\(i\\) we have \\(i\\neq1\\) (otherwise the denominator in (4) would be\nzero, giving a vertical line).  Moreover \\(k+1-i\\neq0\\) because \\(i\\le k\\).\nHence \\(m_i\\) is finite and non\u2011zero.  Solving \\(m_i=-1\\) yields\n\\(k+1-i=i-1\\), i.e. \\(k=2\\) and \\(i=2\\); this never occurs when \\(k\\ge3\\).\nThus each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because the\nunordered pairs \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) are distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  If \\(a\\le b\\) then \\(b=k+2-a\\) and\n\\((a,b)=Q_b\\); consequently the point lies on \\(L_b\\).  If \\(a\\ge b\\) then\n\\(a=k+2-b\\) and \\((a,b)=P_a\\); hence the point lies on \\(L_a\\).  Thus every\nlattice point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n\\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\).\n\n*Special case \\(k=2\\).*  \n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  Two sunny lines covering it are  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1\\quad\\text{through }(1,1),\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=3x-2\\quad\\text{through }(2,1),\n\\]\n\nwhose slopes are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\), respectively.  Both are sunny and together\ncontain the three points of \\(T_2\\).\n\nConsequently, for **every** \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we have produced\n\\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\); adding the vertical lines (1) yields a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the original\nstatement and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny members.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nWe must decide for which \\(n\\) the whole set \\(S_n\\) can be covered by\n\\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1  Existence for \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y = x,                         &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac32,    &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y = -2x + 5,                   &&\\text{slope }-2,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively the point sets  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich together are exactly the ten points of \\(S_3\\).  Hence \\(k=n=3\\) is\nattainable.\n\n#### 3.2  Impossibility for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny and cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Hypotenuse.*  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points.  Any two distinct points of \\(H\\) lie on a line of\nslope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of\n\\(H\\).  Because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, each line contains exactly one\npoint of \\(H\\).\n\n*Left side.*  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nalso has \\(n\\) points.  A sunny line cannot be vertical, so it meets \\(L\\) in\nat most one point.  As before, each of the \\(n\\) lines must contain exactly\none point of \\(L\\).\n\nConsequently we may label the lines \\(\\ell_i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) so that  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i\\text{ contains }H_i=(i,n+1-i)\\text{ and }L_i=(1,b_i),\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((b_1,\\dots ,b_n)\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nFor \\(i\\ge2\\) the line determined by the two points in (7) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{b_i-(n+1-i)}{1-i}\n    =\\frac{b_i+i-n-1}{1-i}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny we must have \\(m_i\\neq-1\\).  The equality\n\\(m_i=-1\\) is equivalent to \\(b_i=n+2-i\\).  Hence for every\n\\(i\\ge2\\)\n\n\\[\nb_i\\neq n+2-i . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\(n+2-i\\;(2\\le i\\le n)\\) form the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\);\ncondition (9) therefore forces the permutation \\((b_2,\\dots ,b_n)\\) to be a\n*derangement* of \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nNow consider the point \\((1,1)\\in L\\).  It must belong to some line\n\\(\\ell_j\\) with \\(j\\ge2\\) (it cannot be on \\(\\ell_1\\) because \\(\\ell_1\\) already\ncontains the point \\((1,b_1)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq1\\)).  Substituting \\((1,1)\\) into\nthe equation of \\(\\ell_j\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(n+1-j)}{1-j}=m_j,\n\\]\n\nwhich, using (8), simplifies to \\(b_j=1\\).  Hence the unique index\n\\(j\\ge2\\) for which \\(b_j=1\\) is forced to satisfy \\(b_j=1\\).\n\nBecause the numbers \\(b_2,\\dots ,b_n\\) are a permutation of\n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{b_1\\}\\), the value \\(1\\) indeed occurs among\nthem, so such a \\(j\\) exists.\n\nNow look at the **bottom side**\n\n\\[\nB=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_1\\) contains the point \\((a_1,1)\\) with \\(a_1\\neq1\\) (otherwise\n\\(\\ell_1\\) would be vertical).  Each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines\n\\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) can meet \\(B\\) in at most one point, because a\nsunny line cannot be horizontal.  Therefore at most \\(1+(n-1)=n\\) points of\n\\(B\\) can be covered.  Since \\((1,1)\\) already belongs to \\(\\ell_j\\) with\n\\(j\\ge2\\), the total number of points of \\(B\\) covered is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\nBut \\(|B|=n\\); consequently at least one point of the bottom side would remain\nuncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nThus a configuration consisting solely of sunny lines cannot exist when\n\\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Final answer\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) the\n  construction of \u00a7\u202f2 provides \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which\n  are sunny, that cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the explicit family of three sunny lines displayed in\n  \u00a7\u202f3.1 shows that \\(k=n=3\\) is also possible.\n\n* For all \\(n\\ge4\\) the impossibility proof of \u00a7\u202f3.2 shows that a family of\n  \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot cover the required set; hence \\(k=n\\) is not\n  admissible.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{n\\}\\text{ if }n=3\\; } .\n\\]"

